<site xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">112057411</site>	<item>
		<title>James Winn appointed CUO and Head of Office, Brit Re &#8211; Bermuda</title>
		<link>https://www.reinsurancene.ws/james-winn-appointed-cuo-and-head-of-office-brit-re-bermuda/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Kane Wells]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 16:00:10 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Brit]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Brit Re]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[People moves]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.reinsurancene.ws/?p=209275</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>Brit Group Holdings Limited has appointed James Winn as Chief Underwriting Officer and Head of Office at Brit Re &#8211; Bermuda. Winn, currently Managing Director of Property Treaty at Brit in London, is expected to relocate to Bermuda towards the end of 2026, subject to the relevant approvals. He has more than 35 years’ experience [&#8230;]</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/james-winn-appointed-cuo-and-head-of-office-brit-re-bermuda/">James Winn appointed CUO and Head of Office, Brit Re &#8211; Bermuda</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Brit Group Holdings Limited has appointed James Winn as Chief Underwriting Officer and Head of Office at Brit Re &#8211; Bermuda.</p>
<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="alignright wp-image-209276" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/james-winn-brit.jpg" alt="" width="360" height="225" srcset="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/james-winn-brit.jpg 800w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/james-winn-brit-300x188.jpg 300w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/james-winn-brit-768x480.jpg 768w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/james-winn-brit-200x125.jpg 200w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/james-winn-brit-100x63.jpg 100w" sizes="(max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" />Winn, currently Managing Director of Property Treaty at Brit in London, is expected to relocate to Bermuda towards the end of 2026, subject to the relevant approvals.</p>
<p>He has more than 35 years’ experience in North American property treaty across the Lloyd’s and Bermuda markets.</p>
<p>Jonathan Stephenson, the current Head of Office, will hand over to Winn towards the end of the year as part of a planned transition.</p>
<p>Brit credited Stephenson with building the business to more than $250 million in third-party premium and establishing a robust underwriting platform to support an expanding product suite, most recently with the launch of US direct and facultative (D&amp;F).</p><div class="reins-in-every-article reins-entity-placement" id="reins-898350218"><div id="reins-731858952" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><a href="https://www.guycarp.com/insights/renewal-hub.html" aria-label="Marsh Re - New name. Expanded vision"><img decoding="async" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1.png" alt="Marsh Re - New name. Expanded vision"  srcset="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1.png 1440w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-300x37.png 300w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-1024x127.png 1024w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-768x95.png 768w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-200x25.png 200w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-100x12.png 100w" sizes="(max-width: 1440px) 100vw, 1440px" width="720" height="89"   /></a></div></div>
<p>Alongside Winn’s appointment, Sam Lewis and Jolie Broomhead, both assistant vice presidents, will relocate from their respective London teams to support Brit Re’s growth in casualty treaty and D&amp;F, respectively.</p>
<p>Jon Sullivan, Group Chief Underwriting Officer at Brit, commented, “We’re delighted that James has agreed to take on the leadership of our Bermuda platform as we look to the next phase of its growth.</p>
<p>&#8220;Since its launch two years ago, Brit Re has grown significantly and we continue to be excited by the opportunity to further build the Brit brand in Bermuda.</p>
<p>&#8220;I would also like to thank Jonathan, who has played a pivotal role in establishing the platform we have today and we are pleased that he will be with us until early 2027 to ensure a seamless handover.”</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/james-winn-appointed-cuo-and-head-of-office-brit-re-bermuda/">James Winn appointed CUO and Head of Office, Brit Re &#8211; Bermuda</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">209275</post-id>	</item>
		<item>
		<title>Marsh leans into AI growth but warns of systemic cyber exposure: CEO Doyle</title>
		<link>https://www.reinsurancene.ws/marsh-leans-into-ai-growth-but-warns-of-systemic-cyber-exposure-ceo-doyle/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Kassandra Jimenez-Sanchez]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:30:28 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Artificial intelligence in insurance and reinsurance news]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Marsh news]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.reinsurancene.ws/?p=209264</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>The rapid adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) carries systemic threats that require coordinated industry oversight as its implementation moves to an immediate operational driver across the re/insurance market, Marsh’s President and CEO John Doyle has commented. Speaking on the broker’s strategic positioning, Doyle emphasised that AI is already reshaping risk advisory, client engagement, and internal [&#8230;]</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/marsh-leans-into-ai-growth-but-warns-of-systemic-cyber-exposure-ceo-doyle/">Marsh leans into AI growth but warns of systemic cyber exposure: CEO Doyle</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>The rapid adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) carries systemic threats that require coordinated industry oversight as its implementation moves to an immediate operational driver across the re/insurance market, Marsh’s President and CEO John Doyle has commented.</p>
<p><img decoding="async" class="alignright wp-image-185418" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/marsh-logo-newest.jpg" alt="" width="360" height="225" srcset="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/marsh-logo-newest.jpg 800w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/marsh-logo-newest-300x188.jpg 300w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/marsh-logo-newest-768x480.jpg 768w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/marsh-logo-newest-200x125.jpg 200w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/marsh-logo-newest-100x63.jpg 100w" sizes="(max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" />Speaking on the broker’s strategic positioning, Doyle emphasised that AI is already reshaping risk advisory, client engagement, and internal workflows.</p>
<p>However, he warned that unchecked deployment could introduce severe, correlated risks to global markets, specifically via amplified cyber exposure.</p>
<p>While optimistic about the broader macroeconomic benefit of AI, Doyle stressed that deployment must be paired with strict governance to avert irreversible damage.</p>
<p>“When it comes to AI, we believe it will be good for our country and the global economy. However, we need to deploy the technology responsibly to avoid potentially severe, systemic, or irreversible risks. We&#8217;re particularly concerned about the potential for heightened cyber exposure from AI, and we are working with governments, insurance companies, and businesses to create awareness and resilience to this risk,” he stated.</p><div class="reins-in-every-article reins-entity-placement" id="reins-898350218"><div id="reins-731858952" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><a href="https://www.guycarp.com/insights/renewal-hub.html" aria-label="Marsh Re - New name. Expanded vision"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1.png" alt="Marsh Re - New name. Expanded vision"  srcset="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1.png 1440w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-300x37.png 300w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-1024x127.png 1024w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-768x95.png 768w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-200x25.png 200w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-100x12.png 100w" sizes="(max-width: 1440px) 100vw, 1440px" width="720" height="89"   /></a></div></div>
<p>The executive affirmed that Marsh plans to take an assertive stance in guiding clients through both sides of the ledger: mitigating AI-related liabilities while capitalising on the technological upside.</p>
<p>He said: “Responsible AI implementation begins with the developers, but businesses and governments have a role to play in ensuring safeguards are in place. Marsh will be active in helping clients manage the risks and capture the opportunities from AI.</p>
<p>&#8220;Turning to AI in our business, we&#8217;re not thinking about it as something that will be a factor in our business. It already is a factor in our business. It&#8217;s enhancing our client relationships, how we manage operations, and how colleagues work every day.&#8221;</p>
<p>Marsh is focusing on several areas where AI is driving growth, including building AI-enabled products, consulting on AI transformation, serving the digital infrastructure ecosystem, and adding to capabilities and productivity for its producers and consultants.</p>
<p>“We&#8217;ve already deployed several AI-enabled products for clients that deliver valuable insights. These solutions tap into our large and expanding proprietary data sets in risk, health, and talent,” Doyle concluded.</p>
<p>The CEO comments follow the release of<a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/marsh-grows-q326-revenue-8-as-reinsurance-generates-374m/"> Marsh&#8217;s financial results for the third quarter of 2026.</a> The company&#8217;s revenue was $6.9 billion, an increase of 8% year on year and 5% on an underlying basis.</p>
<p>Its reinsurance arm, Marsh Re, recorded a 6% revenue decline in the quarter, to $374 million, down from $398 million a year earlier, on both a GAAP and underlying basis.</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/marsh-leans-into-ai-growth-but-warns-of-systemic-cyber-exposure-ceo-doyle/">Marsh leans into AI growth but warns of systemic cyber exposure: CEO Doyle</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">209264</post-id>	</item>
		<item>
		<title>ACIC&#8217;s ACES Specialty Insurance Company gets approval to underwrite E&#038;S insurance</title>
		<link>https://www.reinsurancene.ws/acics-aces-specialty-insurance-company-gets-approval-to-underwrite-es-insurance/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Saumya Jain]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:00:41 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ACES Specialty Insurance Company]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ACIC]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[American Coastal]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[excess & surplus lines]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.reinsurancene.ws/?p=208827</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>ACES Specialty Insurance Company (ACES), a newly formed subsidiary of American Coastal Insurance Corporation (ACIC), the insurance holding company of American Coastal Insurance Company, has received a Certificate of Authority from the Arizona Department of Insurance and Financial Institutions to conduct transactions in excess and surplus (E&#38;S) lines. ACIC initially plans to underwrite catastrophe-exposed commercial [&#8230;]</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/acics-aces-specialty-insurance-company-gets-approval-to-underwrite-es-insurance/">ACIC&#8217;s ACES Specialty Insurance Company gets approval to underwrite E&amp;S insurance</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>ACES Specialty Insurance Company (ACES), a newly formed subsidiary of American Coastal Insurance Corporation (ACIC), the insurance holding company of American Coastal Insurance Company, has received a Certificate of Authority from the Arizona Department of Insurance and Financial Institutions to conduct transactions in excess and surplus (E&amp;S) lines.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignright wp-image-204802" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/american-coastal-logo-new.png" alt="" width="360" height="225" srcset="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/american-coastal-logo-new.png 800w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/american-coastal-logo-new-300x188.png 300w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/american-coastal-logo-new-768x480.png 768w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/american-coastal-logo-new-200x125.png 200w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/american-coastal-logo-new-100x63.png 100w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" />ACIC initially plans to underwrite catastrophe-exposed commercial E&amp;S property business through ACES in Florida, Texas, and South Carolina, building on AmCoastal’s experience in coastal commercial property.</p>
<p>The new insurer expects to begin writing business on or about 1 December, 2026, subject to customary regulatory, rating agency, and other operational requirements.</p>
<p>Brad Martz, President and Chief Executive Officer, ACIC, commented, &#8220;Receiving ACES’s Certificate of Authority is an important milestone for American Coastal and marks the start of a bold new chapter for our organization’s long-term strategy. With ACES, we can now serve a much wider range of commercial property risks in markets we know well.</p>
<p>&#8220;We built this platform along with our in-house managing general agency, Skyway Underwriters, carefully and with purpose, and we’re excited to put it to work for our agents, policyholders, and shareholders.&#8221;</p><div class="reins-in-every-article reins-entity-placement" id="reins-898350218"><div id="reins-2762490782" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><a href="https://www.willisre.com" aria-label="Willis Re"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/willis-re-728x90-sep2026.gif" alt="Willis Re"  width="728" height="90"   /></a></div></div>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/acics-aces-specialty-insurance-company-gets-approval-to-underwrite-es-insurance/">ACIC&#8217;s ACES Specialty Insurance Company gets approval to underwrite E&amp;S insurance</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">208827</post-id>	</item>
		<item>
		<title>Antares Global names Meera Rajoo-Oakley as Chief Actuary of Commercial</title>
		<link>https://www.reinsurancene.ws/antares-global-names-meera-rajoo-oakley-as-chief-actuary-of-commercial/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Saumya Jain]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 14:00:44 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Antares Global]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[People moves]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.reinsurancene.ws/?p=209215</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>Antares Global, an international specialty insurance and reinsurance group, has promoted Meera Rajoo-Oakley to the newly created role of Chief Actuary of Commercial. The move aims to bring pricing, reserving and exposure management for Antares&#8217; commercial division under a single leader. In her new role, Rajoo-Oakley will report directly to Mark Graham, Commercial Chief Executive [&#8230;]</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/antares-global-names-meera-rajoo-oakley-as-chief-actuary-of-commercial/">Antares Global names Meera Rajoo-Oakley as Chief Actuary of Commercial</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Antares Global, an international specialty insurance and reinsurance group, has promoted Meera Rajoo-Oakley to the newly created role of Chief Actuary of Commercial.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignright wp-image-173188" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/antares-logo-qic.jpg" alt="antares logo" width="340" height="213" srcset="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/antares-logo-qic.jpg 800w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/antares-logo-qic-300x188.jpg 300w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/antares-logo-qic-768x480.jpg 768w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/antares-logo-qic-200x125.jpg 200w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/antares-logo-qic-100x63.jpg 100w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 340px) 100vw, 340px" />The move aims to bring pricing, reserving and exposure management for Antares&#8217; commercial division under a single leader.</p>
<p>In her new role, Rajoo-Oakley will report directly to Mark Graham, Commercial Chief Executive Officer for Antares.</p>
<p>Her remit includes bringing strategic oversight of the actuarial and exposure management functions across the business, which comprises Lloyd’s Syndicate 1274 and Antares Re.</p>
<p>Rajoo-Oakley has over 18 years of actuarial experience across the UK and international reinsurance and insurance market. She joined Antares Global in 2017 and has served as Chief Actuary of its Lloyd’s syndicate since 2020.</p><div class="reins-in-every-article reins-entity-placement" id="reins-898350218"><div id="reins-2762490782" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><a href="https://www.willisre.com" aria-label="Willis Re"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/willis-re-728x90-sep2026.gif" alt="Willis Re"  width="728" height="90"   /></a></div></div>
<p>Prior to this, she held roles at Gable Insurance, Direct Line Group and Grant Thornton, and currently is also Chair of the Committee of Actuaries in the Lloyd’s Market.</p>
<p>Graham commented on the appointment, &#8220;Bringing reserving, pricing and exposure management together under Meera’s leadership will enable us to streamline our operations, strengthen the specialist support available to our underwriting and claims teams, and drive the continued development of our Commercial platform.</p>
<p>&#8220;Meera has made a significant contribution to our syndicate’s success during her almost 10 years with the business; and her appointment reinforces our commitment to developing talent and providing clear pathways for professional progression.</p>
<p>&#8220;Her deep actuarial expertise, strong leadership credentials and detailed understanding of our Commercial business also make her ideally placed to take on this role.&#8221;</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/antares-global-names-meera-rajoo-oakley-as-chief-actuary-of-commercial/">Antares Global names Meera Rajoo-Oakley as Chief Actuary of Commercial</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">209215</post-id>	</item>
		<item>
		<title>UK liability claims growth could rise further as legal reforms are reviewed, says Swiss Re</title>
		<link>https://www.reinsurancene.ws/uk-liability-claims-growth-could-rise-further-as-legal-reforms-are-reviewed-says-swiss-re/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Taylor Mixides]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:00:38 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Artificial intelligence in insurance and reinsurance news]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Liability Insurance]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[regulation]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Swiss Re Institute]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Swiss Re news]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.reinsurancene.ws/?p=209210</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>Swiss Re, a global reinsurance company, has warned that liability insurance claims in the UK could face further cost pressures as the government considers reforms to litigation funding, collective redress and product liability. In a report published by the Swiss Re Institute, Senior Economist Arnaud Vanolli examines how legal and regulatory developments could affect claims [&#8230;]</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/uk-liability-claims-growth-could-rise-further-as-legal-reforms-are-reviewed-says-swiss-re/">UK liability claims growth could rise further as legal reforms are reviewed, says Swiss Re</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Swiss Re, a global reinsurance company, has warned that liability insurance claims in the UK could face further cost pressures as the government considers reforms to litigation funding, collective redress and product liability.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignright wp-image-114664" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/swiss-re-logo-300x180.png" alt="swiss-re-logo" width="360" height="216" srcset="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/swiss-re-logo-300x180.png 300w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/swiss-re-logo-768x460.png 768w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/swiss-re-logo-200x120.png 200w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/swiss-re-logo-100x60.png 100w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/swiss-re-logo.png 1002w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" />In a report published by the Swiss Re Institute, Senior Economist Arnaud Vanolli examines how legal and regulatory developments could affect claims growth, insurers and businesses, and highlights the potential role of safeguards in balancing access to justice with economic considerations.</p>
<p>According to Swiss Re, non-economic factors added around five to six percentage points to UK liability claims growth in 2024, beyond the increase explained by macroeconomic conditions.</p>
<p>The company estimates that these factors have accounted for approximately half of liability claims growth since 2020, marking the fifth consecutive year of elevated liability excess inflation.</p>
<p>Swiss Re attributes this trend partly to changes in legislation, legal procedures and litigation practices. It notes that third-party litigation funding, which enables claimants to pursue legal action in return for a share of any compensation or settlement, can make more cases financially viable. However, the company says such arrangements may also reduce incentives for early settlements in some circumstances.</p><div class="reins-in-every-article reins-entity-placement" id="reins-898350218"><div id="reins-2762490782" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><a href="https://www.willisre.com" aria-label="Willis Re"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/willis-re-728x90-sep2026.gif" alt="Willis Re"  width="728" height="90"   /></a></div></div>
<p>Collective redress mechanisms are another area of focus for Swiss Re Institute. Under opt-out arrangements, eligible claimants are automatically included in a group action unless they choose to withdraw, potentially increasing the number of people involved and the overall value of claims. The company highlights the development of collective actions in UK competition law following the Supreme Court&#8217;s 2020 ruling in Merricks v Mastercard as an example of how the legal framework has evolved.</p>
<p>Swiss Re also points to the Building Safety Act 2022, which extended potential liability in the construction and property sectors, as a factor that may have contributed to claims inflation. It adds that the development of litigation funding and claimant recruitment practices has also played a role.</p>
<p>The company says several regulatory developments could influence the future direction of UK liability claims. These include the government&#8217;s plans to reverse the effect of the 2023 PACCAR ruling on certain litigation funding agreements, reviews of collective action procedures and proposals to modernise product liability legislation to address risks associated with digital technologies and artificial intelligence.</p>
<p>The government has stated its intention to support access to justice while promoting fairness and transparency in litigation funding. However, Swiss Re notes that the impact of potential reforms will depend on their final design and implementation. At the time of the report, legislation to reverse the PACCAR decision remained pending.</p>
<p>Swiss Re says a reversal could improve the economics of litigation funding and lead to increased legal activity. It also highlights the possibility that broader opt-out collective action frameworks could increase the size of claimant groups and extend claims activity into additional sectors.</p>
<p>Product liability reform presents a separate consideration. The UK is reviewing legislation introduced in 1987 to address harm arising from digital technologies and AI. According to Swiss Re Institute, any resulting change in insurers&#8217; exposure will depend on how the revised framework balances consumer protection with the interests of businesses developing products and technologies.</p>
<p>The company identifies public liability, product liability, professional liability and cyber insurance as areas that could be particularly affected by changes to collective redress and product liability rules. Construction, engineering, AI and technology are among the sectors where the impact could be significant. Environmental liability could also generate large claims, while employers&#8217; liability and medical malpractice may be less exposed because claims in these areas tend to be more individual in nature.</p>
<p>Swiss Re warns that higher litigation and settlement costs could have wider economic consequences, potentially affecting business investment, research and development, competitiveness and the cost of goods and services. However, it acknowledges that these considerations must be weighed against the potential benefits of improving access to compensation for consumers.</p>
<p>The company argues that regulatory safeguards could help manage the potential impact on liability claims inflation. These include greater transparency around litigation funding arrangements, preserving claimants&#8217; control over litigation and settlement decisions, ensuring that claimants remain the primary beneficiaries of settlements, and maintaining proportionality between compensation and legal costs.</p>
<p>Swiss Re also highlights alternative dispute resolution as a possible way to improve access to redress without requiring court proceedings or litigation funding. It notes that the Civil Justice Council has recommended a light-touch approach to litigation funding regulation, including disclosure requirements and measures to protect claimants&#8217; interests.</p>
<p>According to Swiss Re, the central policy challenge is to balance access to justice with a proportionate legal framework that supports consumer protection, business investment and economic growth. The company concludes that the direction and scale of future liability claims inflation will depend on how the proposed reforms are implemented and whether appropriate safeguards are established.</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/uk-liability-claims-growth-could-rise-further-as-legal-reforms-are-reviewed-says-swiss-re/">UK liability claims growth could rise further as legal reforms are reviewed, says Swiss Re</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">209210</post-id>	</item>
		<item>
		<title>Premia Re sells Alan Gray to Lewis &#038; Ellis</title>
		<link>https://www.reinsurancene.ws/premia-re-sells-alan-gray-to-lewis-ellis/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Saumya Jain]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 12:30:07 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Alan Gray LLC]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[M&A news]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Mergers & acquisitions news]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Permira]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.reinsurancene.ws/?p=209190</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>Reinsurance run-off group Premia Holdings Ltd. has sold its international claims, audit and risk management advisory firm, Alan Gray LLC and A.G. Risk Management, Inc., to Lewis &#38; Ellis, Inc., a Novacap portfolio company. Alan Gray was founded in 1988 to serve insurers, reinsurers, law firms, and other insurance market participants, and was acquired by [&#8230;]</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/premia-re-sells-alan-gray-to-lewis-ellis/">Premia Re sells Alan Gray to Lewis &amp; Ellis</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Reinsurance run-off group Premia Holdings Ltd. has sold its international claims, audit and risk management advisory firm, Alan Gray LLC and A.G. Risk Management, Inc., to Lewis &amp; Ellis, Inc., a Novacap portfolio company.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignright wp-image-54223" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/deal-handshake-mergers-and-acquisitions.jpg" alt="" width="360" height="201" srcset="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/deal-handshake-mergers-and-acquisitions.jpg 479w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/deal-handshake-mergers-and-acquisitions-300x167.jpg 300w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" />Alan Gray was founded in 1988 to serve insurers, reinsurers, law firms, and other insurance market participants, and was <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/premia-holdings-acquires-alan-gray-llc/">acquired by Premia in 2018</a>.</p>
<p>The firm provides expertise in complex claims, litigation and legacy insurance liabilities, complementing Lewis &amp; Ellis’s actuarial and regulatory practices across life, health and property &amp; casualty insurance.</p>
<p>Bill O’Farrell, Chief Executive Officer, Premia, commented, &#8220;Alan Gray’s strength has always been its people and the confidence clients place in their expertise.</p>
<p>&#8220;Lewis &amp; Ellis brings complementary capabilities and a shared commitment to high-quality client service. We believe this combination provides a strong foundation for Alan Gray’s next chapter, giving its professionals broader resources to support their clients.&#8221;</p><div class="reins-in-every-article reins-entity-placement" id="reins-898350218"><div id="reins-731858952" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><a href="https://www.guycarp.com/insights/renewal-hub.html" aria-label="Marsh Re - New name. Expanded vision"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1.png" alt="Marsh Re - New name. Expanded vision"  srcset="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1.png 1440w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-300x37.png 300w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-1024x127.png 1024w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-768x95.png 768w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-200x25.png 200w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-100x12.png 100w" sizes="(max-width: 1440px) 100vw, 1440px" width="720" height="89"   /></a></div></div>
<p>The transaction is Lewis &amp; Ellis’s third add-on acquisition since partnering with Novacap in 2023, marking a milestone in the firm’s evolution into a diversified insurance services platform.</p>
<p>Through this acquisition, Lewis &amp; Ellis aims to broaden its ability to help clients understand and manage insurance risk across pricing, reserving, complex claims, forensic accounting, legal expense management, and run-off and estate administration.</p>
<p>Cabe Chadick, President &amp; Managing Principal of Lewis &amp; Ellis, added, &#8220;Actuarial expertise remains the foundation of Lewis &amp; Ellis. Our clients face increasingly complex challenges that require multidisciplinary solutions. Bringing Alan Gray into Lewis &amp; Ellis connects our pricing, reserving and regulatory work with deep claims and operational experience.</p>
<p>&#8220;This is an important step in our long-term strategy to build a diversified insurance services platform that serves clients across the risk and insurance value chain, while remaining grounded in the technical rigor that has always defined our firm.&#8221;</p>
<p>Premia&#8217;s exclusive financial advisor was Houlihan Lokey, and its shareholder, Kelso &amp; Company, and Mayer Brown served as legal counsel. Meanwhile, Foley &amp; Lardner served as legal counsel to Lewis &amp; Ellis.</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/premia-re-sells-alan-gray-to-lewis-ellis/">Premia Re sells Alan Gray to Lewis &amp; Ellis</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">209190</post-id>	</item>
		<item>
		<title>Marsh grows Q3&#8217;26 revenue 8% as reinsurance generates $374m</title>
		<link>https://www.reinsurancene.ws/marsh-grows-q326-revenue-8-as-reinsurance-generates-374m/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Kane Wells]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 12:07:17 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Marsh Re]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Results news]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.reinsurancene.ws/?p=209236</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>Marsh Re has recorded a 6% decline in Q3 2026 revenue to $374 million, down from $398 million a year earlier, on both a GAAP and underlying basis, as the wider group reported growth across its other main business lines. The reinsurance business, previously reported under the Guy Carpenter name, was rebranded as Marsh Re [&#8230;]</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/marsh-grows-q326-revenue-8-as-reinsurance-generates-374m/">Marsh grows Q3&#8217;26 revenue 8% as reinsurance generates $374m</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Marsh Re has recorded a 6% decline in Q3 2026 revenue to $374 million, down from $398 million a year earlier, on both a GAAP and underlying basis, as the wider group reported growth across its other main business lines.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignright wp-image-207609" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/marsh-re-logo.jpg" alt="" width="360" height="225" srcset="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/marsh-re-logo.jpg 800w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/marsh-re-logo-300x188.jpg 300w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/marsh-re-logo-768x480.jpg 768w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/marsh-re-logo-200x125.jpg 200w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/marsh-re-logo-100x63.jpg 100w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" />The reinsurance business, previously reported under the Guy Carpenter name, was rebranded as Marsh Re following Marsh’s January 2026 brand transition.</p>
<p>For the first nine months of 2026, Reinsurance revenue was $2.278 billion, compared with $2.281 billion in the same period last year, while underlying revenue declined 1%.</p>
<p>Meanwhile, the wider Risk and Insurance Services division, which includes Marsh Re, reported Q3 2026 revenue of $4.2 billion, up 7% year on year and 3% on an underlying basis.</p>
<p>Nine-month revenue for the division rose 6% to $14.1 billion, with underlying growth of 3%.</p><div class="reins-in-every-article reins-entity-placement" id="reins-898350218"><div id="reins-731858952" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><a href="https://www.guycarp.com/insights/renewal-hub.html" aria-label="Marsh Re - New name. Expanded vision"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1.png" alt="Marsh Re - New name. Expanded vision"  srcset="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1.png 1440w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-300x37.png 300w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-1024x127.png 1024w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-768x95.png 768w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-200x25.png 200w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/marsh-re-in-article-720x90-1-100x12.png 100w" sizes="(max-width: 1440px) 100vw, 1440px" width="720" height="89"   /></a></div></div>
<p>Notably, within the same division, Marsh Risk, the group’s insurance broking business, generated Q3 2026 revenue of $3.7 billion, an increase of 10% year on year, or 5% on an underlying basis.</p>
<p>Elsewhere, Marsh’s Consulting business reported Q3 2026 revenue of $2.7 billion, up 9% year on year, with underlying growth of 8%. For the first nine months of the year, Consulting revenue rose 10% to $7.8 billion, representing underlying growth of 7%.</p>
<p>Within the Consulting division, Management Consulting revenue rose 14% to $1 billion in Q3 2026, on both a GAAP and underlying basis. People and Investments generated revenue of $1.7 billion, up 6% year on year and 5% on an underlying basis.</p>
<p>At group level, Marsh reported Q3 revenue of $6.9 billion, an increase of 8% year on year and 5% on an underlying basis.</p>
<p>Operating income increased 4% to $1.2 billion in Q3 2026, while adjusted operating income rose 6% to $1.5 billion.</p>
<p>Net income attributable to the firm reached $808 million, while adjusted earnings per share increased 10% to $2.03.</p>
<p>Equity analysts at KBW said adjusted earnings per share beat the consensus estimate of $1.97, based on 24 analyst estimates ranging from $1.92 to $2.00, as well as KBW’s own forecast of $1.99.</p>
<p>The outperformance was driven mainly by stronger-than-expected organic growth and margins in the aforementioned Consulting business.</p>
<p>For the first nine months of 2026, Marsh’s revenue totalled $21.9 billion, an increase of 7% on a GAAP basis and 5% on an underlying basis.</p>
<p>John Doyle, President and CEO, commented, &#8220;Marsh delivered a strong third quarter, generating 5% underlying revenue growth and double-digit EPS growth.</p>
<p>&#8220;We also made significant progress in executing our Thrive program and successfully completed our brand transition.&#8221;</p>
<p>&#8220;These results reflect the strength of our market position, the value we deliver to clients, and the commitment of our colleagues around the world.&#8221;</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/marsh-grows-q326-revenue-8-as-reinsurance-generates-374m/">Marsh grows Q3&#8217;26 revenue 8% as reinsurance generates $374m</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">209236</post-id>	</item>
		<item>
		<title>MacHale to join Clearwater Underwriting as CEO</title>
		<link>https://www.reinsurancene.ws/machale-to-join-clearwater-underwriting-as-ceo/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Kassandra Jimenez-Sanchez]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 11:30:32 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Clearwater UW]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[MGA]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[People moves]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.reinsurancene.ws/?p=209213</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>Clearwater Underwriting, an Aquiline-backed managing general agent (MGA), has announced the appointment of Henry MacHale as Chief Executive Officer, a newly created role. Bringing extensive experience across the specialty insurance market. MacHale will depart Sompo, where he currently serves as Chief Underwriting Officer &#38; Head of Strategy, International Markets, prior to assuming his new position. [&#8230;]</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/machale-to-join-clearwater-underwriting-as-ceo/">MacHale to join Clearwater Underwriting as CEO</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Clearwater Underwriting, an Aquiline-backed managing general agent (MGA), has announced the appointment of Henry MacHale as Chief Executive Officer, a newly created role.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignright wp-image-188279" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/ClearwaterUW_logo.jpg" alt="" width="360" height="225" srcset="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/ClearwaterUW_logo.jpg 800w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/ClearwaterUW_logo-300x188.jpg 300w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/ClearwaterUW_logo-768x480.jpg 768w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/ClearwaterUW_logo-200x125.jpg 200w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/ClearwaterUW_logo-100x63.jpg 100w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" />Bringing extensive experience across the specialty insurance market. MacHale will depart Sompo, where he currently serves as Chief Underwriting Officer &amp; Head of Strategy, International Markets, prior to assuming his new position.</p>
<p>Prior to Sompo, he held senior roles at Aspen, including Portfolio Director for First Party &amp; Specialty lines, CUO for AIUK Insurance, and Group Chief Strategy Officer, where he was closely involved in the private equity-backed liquidity event process.</p>
<p>MacHale&#8217;s appointment marks an important step in Clearwater’s development as it looks to build on the platform it has established and pursue its ambitions for the business.</p>
<p>Moving beyond its foundational Marine focus, Clearwater aims to build a leading multi-line specialty underwriting business, expanding into a broader range of specialty and niche classes.</p><div class="reins-in-every-article reins-entity-placement" id="reins-898350218"><div id="reins-2762490782" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><a href="https://www.willisre.com" aria-label="Willis Re"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/willis-re-728x90-sep2026.gif" alt="Willis Re"  width="728" height="90"   /></a></div></div>
<p>The business plans to grow through a combination of new underwriting capabilities, expansion into selected classes and markets, and targeted M&amp;A, supported by strong broker and capacity relationships.</p>
<p>Through this expansion, maintaining underwriting discipline and a focus on sustainable, long-term profitability will remain central to its growth, the firm highlighted.</p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">Richard Young, Chief Underwriting Officer at Clearwater Underwriting, said: “The appointment of Henry as our Chief Executive Officer marks an important milestone for Clearwater. We have built a strong business with an experienced team and see significant opportunity for its continued development. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">“Henry’s understanding of the specialty insurance market, commercial perspective and experience of businesses at different stages of growth and evolution make him a natural fit for Clearwater. We look forward to welcoming Henry to the team and to working with him as we continue to scale the business and broaden the opportunities available to Clearwater.”</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">MacHale, who is</span><span style="font-weight: 400;"> expected to take up the role in the second quarter of 2027, commented:</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">“I am delighted to have the opportunity to join Clearwater as CEO. I have been hugely impressed by the quality of the business, the expertise of the team and the ambition for its future. I look forward to working alongside the team to build on these foundations and deliver on Clearwater’s ambitions for the next phase of its development. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400;">“I am extremely grateful to Sompo for the opportunities and experience I have gained during my time with the organisation. It has been a privilege to work as part of a leading global balance-sheet business, and I wish the team all the best for the ongoing growth and success of the business.” </span></p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/machale-to-join-clearwater-underwriting-as-ceo/">MacHale to join Clearwater Underwriting as CEO</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">209213</post-id>	</item>
		<item>
		<title>KCC estimates insured losses from Hurricane Isaias at $3bn</title>
		<link>https://www.reinsurancene.ws/kcc-estimates-insured-losses-from-hurricane-isaias-at-3bn/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Kane Wells]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 11:00:06 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Catastrophe losses]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Hurricane Isaias]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Karen Clark & Company]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[KCC]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[loss estimates]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Losses]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.reinsurancene.ws/?p=209223</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>Karen Clark &#38; Company (KCC) has estimated that the privately insured loss from Hurricane Isaias will be $3 billion in the US from wind and storm surge. Based on KCC’s high-resolution US Hurricane Model, the estimate covers damage to residential, commercial and industrial properties, as well as automobiles and business interruption. It excludes boats, offshore [&#8230;]</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/kcc-estimates-insured-losses-from-hurricane-isaias-at-3bn/">KCC estimates insured losses from Hurricane Isaias at $3bn</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Karen Clark &amp; Company (KCC) has estimated that the privately insured loss from Hurricane Isaias will be $3 billion in the US from wind and storm surge.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignright wp-image-170177" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/karen-clark-and-company-new.jpg" alt="" width="360" height="225" srcset="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/karen-clark-and-company-new.jpg 800w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/karen-clark-and-company-new-300x188.jpg 300w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/karen-clark-and-company-new-768x480.jpg 768w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/karen-clark-and-company-new-200x125.jpg 200w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/karen-clark-and-company-new-100x63.jpg 100w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" />Based on KCC’s high-resolution US Hurricane Model, the estimate covers damage to residential, commercial and industrial properties, as well as automobiles and business interruption.</p>
<p>It excludes boats, offshore properties and losses covered by the National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP).</p>
<p>Isaias made landfall in Destin, Florida, as a Category 2 hurricane with 105-mph winds and a peak storm surge of about 8 feet.</p>
<p>It was the ninth named storm of the 2026 Atlantic hurricane season but the first to reach hurricane strength.</p><div class="reins-in-every-article reins-entity-placement" id="reins-898350218"><div id="reins-2762490782" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><a href="https://www.willisre.com" aria-label="Willis Re"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/willis-re-728x90-sep2026.gif" alt="Willis Re"  width="728" height="90"   /></a></div></div>
<p>KCC observed that the most severe impacts were concentrated in the coastal cities and towns of the Florida Panhandle, like Destin, Pensacola Beach, and Navarre. Major damage has also been observed in towns around Pensacola Bay, such as Milton and Pace.</p>
<p>Meanwhile, power outages were widespread across Florida and Alabama and extended into western Georgia.</p>
<p>Other firms have also weighed in with their own estimates of the financial impact.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/verisk-estimates-isaias-insured-losses-at-1-2bn-driven-largely-by-wind-damage/">Verisk’s Catastrophe and Risk Solutions business has put insured industry losses to onshore property at between $1 billion and $2 billion</a>, with wind damage expected to account for the vast majority of claims.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/cotality-estimates-up-to-3bn-in-insured-losses-from-isaias-wind-and-storm-surge/">Cotality estimates insured losses from wind and storm surge at between $2 billion and $3 billion</a>, while Euler ILS Partners has placed its initial estimate of industry losses at between $2 billion and $4 billion.</p>
<p>Beyond insured losses, Moody’s expects Isaias to deliver a modest regional economic shock, forecasting a $1 billion to $2 billion reduction in economic output.</p>
<p>AccuWeather has issued a broader estimate, putting total damage and economic losses across the Gulf and eastern United States at between $16 billion and $20 billion.</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/kcc-estimates-insured-losses-from-hurricane-isaias-at-3bn/">KCC estimates insured losses from Hurricane Isaias at $3bn</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">209223</post-id>	</item>
		<item>
		<title>LM Re appoints Fredrik Øyan as Market Manager, Nordics</title>
		<link>https://www.reinsurancene.ws/lm-re-appoints-fredrik-oyan-as-market-manager-nordics/</link>
		
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Beth Musselwhite]]></dc:creator>
		<pubDate>Thu, 15 Oct 2026 10:30:33 +0000</pubDate>
				<category><![CDATA[News]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Liberty Mutual Re]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[LM Re]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[People moves]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.reinsurancene.ws/?p=209211</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>Liberty Mutual Reinsurance (LM Re), part of Liberty Mutual Insurance Group, has announced the appointment of Fredrik H. Øyan as Market Manager, Nordics, effective 1 November 2026. Based in Liberty’s Oslo office, Øyan will work closely with underwriting teams in Cologne, London and Madrid to strengthen collaboration across the Nordic markets and support the continued [&#8230;]</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/lm-re-appoints-fredrik-oyan-as-market-manager-nordics/">LM Re appoints Fredrik Øyan as Market Manager, Nordics</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>Liberty Mutual Reinsurance (LM Re), part of Liberty Mutual Insurance Group, has announced the appointment of Fredrik H. Øyan as Market Manager, Nordics, effective 1 November 2026.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignright wp-image-160933" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/liberty-mutual-re-logo.jpg" alt="Liberty Mutual Reinsurance" width="360" height="225" srcset="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/liberty-mutual-re-logo.jpg 800w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/liberty-mutual-re-logo-300x188.jpg 300w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/liberty-mutual-re-logo-768x480.jpg 768w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/liberty-mutual-re-logo-200x125.jpg 200w, https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/liberty-mutual-re-logo-100x63.jpg 100w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" />Based in Liberty’s Oslo office, Øyan will work closely with underwriting teams in Cologne, London and Madrid to strengthen collaboration across the Nordic markets and support the continued growth of the business in Northern Europe.</p>
<p>He brings more than 20 years of experience in the Nordic insurance and reinsurance markets, with expertise in both underwriting and broking.</p>
<p>Øyan has held overall underwriting responsibility for property and casualty lines of business for more than a decade, while also leading reinsurance purchasing across all lines.</p>
<p>He joins LM Re from Protector Forsikring ASA, where he played a central role in its expansion into Sweden, Denmark and the UK. He has also built longstanding relationships with leading insurers, reinsurers and brokers across the Nordics and key international reinsurance hubs, including London, Zurich, Cologne and Munich.</p><div class="reins-in-every-article reins-entity-placement" id="reins-898350218"><div id="reins-2762490782" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><a href="https://www.willisre.com" aria-label="Willis Re"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/willis-re-728x90-sep2026.gif" alt="Willis Re"  width="728" height="90"   /></a></div></div>
<p>Andreas Blosche, Head of Northern Europe at Liberty Mutual Re, said, “We are delighted to welcome Fredrik to Liberty Mutual Re. His extensive market knowledge, strong industry relationships, and broad experience across underwriting and reinsurance purchasing make him an excellent addition to our team. Fredrik will play an important role in strengthening our presence and partnerships across the Nordic region.”</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws/lm-re-appoints-fredrik-oyan-as-market-manager-nordics/">LM Re appoints Fredrik Øyan as Market Manager, Nordics</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.reinsurancene.ws">ReinsuranceNe.ws</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
		<post-id xmlns="com-wordpress:feed-additions:1">209211</post-id>	</item>
